  "prompt_text": "The figure compares two broad scenarios for the spread of early domestic animals across Eurasia and into the Americas. Both panels are world maps centered on Europe and Asia, with arrows showing movement routes for different species: horse, dog, pig, goat, and cattle.\n\nIn the upper map, movement is concentrated around western and central Asia. Goats appear to spread eastward from the Fertile Crescent toward the Indus Valley, while pigs and possibly other livestock move between the Near East, Iberia, and the Newgrange region. Light blue arrows around the Botai and central Asia suggest uncertain horse-related movements, marked with question marks. Dogs are shown farther north, with long routes extending across Siberia and into North America.\n\nThe lower map shows a much wider, later-looking dispersal pattern. Dogs again span Siberia and cross into North America, but they also continue across the Atlantic-facing north and into the Americas, including South America. Around the Near East and South Asia, horses, pigs, goats, and cattle spread in overlapping but distinct directions, implying multiple domestication centers or exchange networks. Overall, the figure emphasizes that different domestic animals followed different geographic pathways, with some routes remaining uncertain.",
